    "hasAnalysis": true,
    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Wild Style (1983) revolutionized hip-hop cinema by capturing New York's explosive graffiti and rap scene just as it was going mainstream. Director Charlie Ahearn cast real artists like Fab 5 Freddy, Lee Quiñones, and Lady Pink rather than actors, giving the film raw authenticity. The loose plot following graffiti artist Zoro (Quiñones) takes a backseat to electrifying performances from Grandmaster Flash, Cold Crush Brothers, and Rock Steady Crew. This wasn't just entertainment - it was the first major film to document hip-hop culture's four elements: MCing, DJing, breaking, and graffiti art."
      },
      {
