      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The cultural impact of Wild Style rippled through decades of hip-hop films. <a href=\"/movie/65\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"65\">8 Mile</a> (2002) borrowed its \"underground artist going legitimate\" narrative, while <a href=\"/movie/292\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"292\">Dave Chappelle's Block Party</a> (2005) echoed its celebration of live performance and community. The film's gritty street footage influenced everything from Spike Lee's <a href=\"/movie/925\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"925\">Do the Right Thing</a> (1989) to the French urban drama <a href=\"/movie/406\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"406\">La Haine</a> (1995)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Beyond hip-hop, Wild Style helped legitimize street art in mainstream culture. Jean-Michel Basquiat, who appears briefly in the film, would soon become a fine art superstar. The film's influence extends to documentaries like <a href=\"/movie/39452\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"39452\">Exit Through the Gift Shop</a> (2010) and fiction films like <a href=\"/movie/41613\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"41613\">Bomb the System</a> (2002), showing how graffiti evolved from vandalism to valued artistic expression. SUBHEAD: Hip-Hop Cinema Evolution"
      },
      {
